ity, the coupling between flows and shape and, in some regimes, the presence of turbulence or flow instabilities. In biological contexts, deformable, curved fluid interfaces such as the cell cortex or lipid bilayers play a key role in morphogenetic processes, cell motility, and signal
ing. Membrane-bound proteins can locally alter mechanical or transport properties and interact with curvature to generate feedback mechanisms [8,11].
On macroscopic scales, fluid sheets can exhibit instabilities, folding, or turbulent behavior driven by external forcing or intrinsic flow dynamics [12–14]. On mesoscopic, planetary scales, layers of clouds at low altitude in the atmosphere may exhibit patterns whose spatial extensions are so large that the curvature of the layer surfaceinduced by the Earth’s curvature—is significant [15]. A unified numerical framework that captures both in-plane and out-of-plane deformations across regimes and scales is essential for understanding such phenomena. Several numerical approaches have been proposed to numerically solve surface hydrodynamics: These include boundary-integral methods [16], immersed-boundary methods [17,18], spectral methods [19], Monte Carlo methods [20], surface-finite-element [21] and finiteelement methods [22,23], for which a large variety of computationaloptimization methods have been developed, including classical [24] and quantum [25,26] algorithms. While each of these techniques is well suited to specific geometries and physical regimes, their applicability is often restricted. Many methods are formulated for closed or simply connected surfaces and focus on low-Reynolds-number flows [21,27]. Others are limited to lower-dimensional manifolds [22]. Other studies [28] based on non-H1 conforming FE spaces, allow for pointwise divergencefree and exactly tangential solutions, and for describing fixed surfaces with a general structure, i.e., which are not necessarily the graph of a function of two variables [4]. These studies have been recently extended to passively moving manifolds [29,30]. In addition, approaches that explicitly couple surface deformation with fluid flow typically rely on creeping-flow assumptions [23]. Moreover, from a software perspective, existing FE implementations are often not distributed as fully documented open-source tools, and they rarely support the inclusion of a large variety of mesh geometries and obstacles.
