2. Mathematical framework
Let us consider a two-dimensional fluid layer embedded in threedimensional space, see Fig. 1. The elements of the fluid flow tangentially to the layer and the layer itself can deform, exhibiting a velocity normal to the layer surface: we will denote the tangential and normal velocities above by v and w respectively. Geometrically speaking, the surface of the layer constitutes a differential manifold, which we will denote by M. The velocity vi is a vector field in the tangent bundle of M, while w is a scalar field on M [1]. The surface tension of the layer is denoted by σ. Choosing—for example—the Monge parametrization [4] to parametrize M, the layer height is given by a function z(x), where x are the coordinates on a domain Ω ∈ R2, see Fig. 1.
In what follows, we will assume that the elastic response of the layer is described by the Helfrich free energy [39]. However, the modular structure of i
r
en
e
allows for implementing other elastic models, see Section 4.
The equations which describe the dynamics of the velocities, tension and shape of the layer are listed in Table 1, and they are Eqs. (10)–(13) [5,40,41]. Eqs. (10)–(13) are a set of PDEs [3], defined on a set Ω with boundary ∂Ω, for the two components vi of the tangent velocity v, the normal velocity w, the surface tension σ, and the fluid shape profile z, where each unknown depends on both the coordinates x on Ω and time t. In Eqs. (10)–(13), ∇ is the covariant derivative, g the metric tensor, b the second fundamental form, H and K the mean and Gaussian curvatures, respectively, [42], and σ the surface tension. In Eq. (11) and Eq. (12), ΔLB is the Laplace-Beltrami operator applied to a vector and scalar field,
given by Eq. (S11) and Eq. (S10), respectively [5]. The vector ̂N is the unit normal vector to Ω, which lives in the Euclidean, three-dimensional space in which Ω is embedded, while n is the normal to a boundary of M, and it belongs to the tangent bundle of M [42], see Fig. 1. Also, ρ
and η are the two-dimensional density and viscosity, respectively, and κ the bending rigidity [41]. It is important to point out that, despite the fact that in i
