3.1. Steady state
At steady state, we set all time derivatives in Eqs. (10)–(13) to zero, and obtain Eqs. (4)–(7) in Table 1, where ω and μ and are given by Eqs. (2) and (3), respectively. In Eq. (5) and in Eq. (6) ΔLB is given by Eq. (S11) and Eq. (S10), respectively. While in Eqs. (4)–(7) the time derivatives vanish, the convective terms which enter in the material derivative of the velocities [2] are present: Together with the terms involving the manifold shape, such terms contribute to the nonlinearity of the PDEs.
We will now consider the steady state in the absence of flows first, and then discuss the steady state in the presence of flows.
3.1.1. Steady state with no flows
In the absence of flows, Eqs. (10)–(13), with vanishing time derivatives and vi = w = 0, reduce to a single PDE which determines the layer shape [6,43,44], i.e., Eq. (1), in which H is given by Eq. (3).
Eqs. (4), (5) and (7) are identically satisfied, and in Eq. (1) ΔLB is given by Eq. (S10). Here, we choose the coordinates of the Monge parametrization, see Section S1.7, and suppose that the surface-tension profile σ is given: as a result, the only unknown of Eq. (1) is the membrane profile z. This reflects a physical situation where, for example, the
intrinsic features of the layer material are such that the surface tension is barely affected by the layer shape. From the mathematical standpoint, Eq. (1) is a fourth-order PDE in z, since H contains up to second-order derivatives of z; see Eqs. (S2) to (S6),
Eq. (1) requires special care when handled with finite-element methods (FEMs). A direct, conforming discretization of a fourth-order PDE such as (1) requires H2-conforming FE spaces. In fact, integrating by parts twice yields a weak formulation containing second derivatives of z, which are square-integrable only if fields are defined in Hp-conforming spaces with p ≥ 2. However, i
r
en
e
is based on the FEniCS library [7], which provides H1-conforming FE spaces only: fields and their first derivatives are square-integrable, but first derivatives can be discontinuous [7]. Consequently, the second derivatives of z appearing in the H2 weak formulation of Eq. (1) are not square-integrable in these spaces, and a direct conforming discretization is not available. In order to overcome this issue, we recast the fourth-order problem as a system of coupled lower-order equations by introducing two auxiliary fields ω and μ, defined in terms of the partial derivatives of z, see Eqs. (2) and (3) in Table 1. We re-express Eq. (1) in terms of ω and μ, so that the resulting equations (1) to (3) involve at most second derivatives of the unknowns z, ω and μ and, after one integration by parts, their weak form involves at most first derivatives of such unknowns. Given that FEniCS spaces are H1 conforming, the weak formulation of these equations is now well posed. Finally, the introduction of the additional field ω allows
