’s solution for a geometry given by a rectangle with a circular hole, where no numerically exact solution exists.
In order to illustrate i
r
en
e
’s predictions for the system dynamics, we focused on a macroscopic example—air flow in a channel with a circular obstacle [59]. First, we considered the dynamics on a fixed manifold. In the absence of an obstacle, this problem reduces to Poiseuille flow [2] on a curved surface, for which we worked out an analytical solution at steady state, which displays laminar flow. i
r
en
e
’s results for the velocity field at the channel outflow converge, as the dynamics reaches steady state, to such analytical solution, see Fig. 13. In the presence of an obstacle in the channel, i
r
en
e
reproduces an inertia-dominated dynamics—the von Kármán vortex street—see Fig. 14 and Movie 1. Interestingly, such von Kármán vortex street on a fixed, curved manifold present some physical applications on larger, planetary scales. In fact, the flow of a cloud layer at low-enough altitudes
over obstacles, such as islands or isolated mountains, may give rise to von Kármán vortex streets, visible in satellite images [72,73]. Given their extension, which may reach over 400 Km [15], these vortex streets may be influenced by the curvature of the Earth, and thus of the cloud layer. As a result, the dynamics of this mesoscale phenomenon may be investigated with i
r
en
e
along the lines of the analysis of the inertia-dominated flow on a fixed, curved manifold, which may provide general guidance in the understanding of its dynamics and meteorological implications. Second, we studied an inertia-dominated air flow on a manifold which is allowed to deform. This corresponds to a flow of air, confined in a two-dimensional, flexible layer composed of rubber-like material, see Fig. 17. For this problem, we combined the IPCS [35] for NS equations, with the CN time-discretization approach [26,36], and demonstrated that this leads to a numerically stable dynamics. Such dynamics shows that the velocity field tangential to the manifold, and the manifold shape, exhibit an inertia-dominated behavior, in which deformations propagate downstream in the shape of intricate wave patterns, see Fig. 17A.
